course: biology
lesson: 346
title: Fruit: how apples, bananas, oranges, strawberries and grapes grow
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A fruit is the part of a plant that grows from a flower and holds its seeds. Cut an apple across the middle and you find a five-pointed star: five little rooms, one for each part of the flower, with up to two pips in each.
explain: Fruit grows in surprising ways. Bananas grow on a giant herb, not a tree, and the fruits curve upwards towards the light. A pineapple is a hundred or more tiny flowers grown together, and a strawberry wears its seeds on the outside.
explain: Fruit also talks to other fruit. A ripe apple gives off a gas called ethylene that tells nearby fruit to ripen, which is why a hard green banana shut in a paper bag with an apple ripens fast, and why one rotten apple spoils the bowl.
explain: And watch the dog: grapes and raisins that are a healthy snack for you can badly damage a dog's kidneys, so if a dog eats some, tell a grown-up straight away.
fact: An apple's seeds sit in five little rooms arranged in a star, with up to ten pips, two in each room, and pears and quinces hide the same star.
fact: A tree grown from an apple pip gives apples that differ from the parent, because each pip has two parents, the tree and the tree whose pollen a bee brought, so orchards grow the same kind by grafting, joining a twig of a good tree onto young roots.
fact: Every Granny Smith tree comes from one seedling found in Australia around 1868 by Maria Ann Smith.
fact: Bananas grow on a giant herb whose trunk is rolled-up leaf stalks; a bunch is made of hands, and each banana is a finger.
fact: Almost all shop bananas are one kind, Cavendish; an older kind, Gros Michel, was nearly wiped out by a plant disease in the 1950s.
fact: Most oranges have about ten segments, each packed with tiny juice bags that grew from hairs inside the flower, but the number changes from orange to orange.
fact: In hot countries oranges can be ripe and sweet while still green, because the skin turns orange only when cool nights break down the green colour.
fact: A strawberry carries about 200 seeds on its outside, each really a tiny fruit, and the red juicy part is the swollen base of the flower; strawberry plants also make new plants from long runners.
fact: Grapes grow on a woody climbing vine that clings with curly tendrils and is pruned hard each winter; raisins and sultanas are dried grapes.
fact: The Great Vine at Hampton Court Palace near London was planted in 1768 and still gives grapes.
fact: Grapes and raisins can badly damage a dog's kidneys even in small amounts; scientists think a natural acid in grapes is to blame.
fact: Ripe fruit gives off ethylene gas, which makes nearby fruit ripen, and shippers keep bananas cold and away from it on long trips, then release the gas in ripening rooms.
fact: A pineapple grows one at a time from the middle of a low spiky plant, takes about a year and a half or more, and is made of a hundred or more tiny flowers grown together; its leafy top can be planted.
exercise: What do you find if you cut an apple across the middle? | A five-pointed star of seed rooms | A neat ring of ten tiny baby apples | A hollow full of juice | One big hard stone
exercise: Why is a tree from an apple pip different from its parent? | Each pip mixes the features of two parent trees | Pips forget which kind of apple they came from once they are buried | Soil changes the kind of apple | Pips only grow crab apples
exercise: How do orchards grow many trees of the same kind of apple? | By grafting a twig of a good tree onto young roots | By planting the pips from their very best apples | By cutting apples in half and burying them in rows | By watering trees with apple juice
exercise: Where did the first Granny Smith apple tree come from? | A seedling found in Australia around 1868 | A royal garden in London, planted for the Queen | A laboratory in America | A wild forest in Russia
exercise: What does a banana grow on? | A giant herb whose trunk is rolled-up leaf stalks | A tall palm tree with a hard wooden trunk and coconuts | A climbing vine | A bush under the ground
exercise: What is a group of bananas in a bunch called? | A hand | A foot | A finger | A family
exercise: Which kind of banana was nearly wiped out by a disease in the 1950s? | Gros Michel | Cavendish | Granny Smith | Gala
exercise: About how many segments does an orange usually have? | About ten | About three | About fifty | Always exactly twelve
exercise: Why can a ripe orange still be green in a hot country like Vietnam? | Nights stay warm, and the skin turns orange only after cool nights | Vietnamese oranges are really a kind of lime that tastes sweet | The oranges were picked too early | Green oranges are painted for tourists
exercise: Where are a strawberry's seeds? | On the outside | In a star in the middle | In a hard stone | Strawberries have no seeds
exercise: Besides seeds, how do strawberry plants make new plants? | With long runners that root where they touch soil | With round bulbs under the ground, the way onions do | With spores like ferns | With their leaves falling off
exercise: What are raisins and sultanas? | Dried grapes | Dried plums | Small cherries | Dried strawberries
exercise: What does a grape vine use to climb? | Curly tendrils | Sticky roots in the air | Thorns like a rose | Nothing, it stands up alone
exercise: When was the Great Vine at Hampton Court planted? | 1768 | 1068 | 1968 | 1868
exercise: What can grapes and raisins do to a dog? | Badly damage its kidneys | Make its coat thick and shiny | Clean its teeth | Nothing at all
exercise: Why does a green banana ripen faster in a bag with an apple? | The apple gives off ethylene gas that makes fruit ripen | The apple slowly shares its own sugar with the banana | The bag keeps the banana in the dark | Apples suck the green colour out of bananas through the skin
exercise: What is a pineapple made of? | A hundred or more tiny flowers grown together | One single huge flower that has swollen up | A pine cone filled with juice | Leaves rolled into a ball
test: In a jungle cartoon, a monkey grabs a banana bunch drooping straight down off a palm tree. What is wrong? | Bananas grow on a giant herb and curve upwards towards the light | Nothing is wrong, because bananas always hang straight down from tall palm trees | Bananas grow under the ground | Bananas grow one at a time on vines
test: Count them: he cuts 3 apples across and every room has 2 pips. How many seed rooms and how many pips? | 15 rooms and 30 pips | 10 rooms and 20 pips | 15 rooms and 15 pips | 30 rooms and 30 pips
test: «gonna plant a Granny Smith pip and get Granny Smiths, easy» What will most likely happen? | The tree will give a different apple, because pips mix two parents | He will get exactly the same Granny Smiths, because a pip always copies its tree | He will get pears | The pip cannot grow at all
test: «whole country only grows one banana, what could go wrong lol» What is the danger? | One disease could wipe out almost all of them | Nothing, having just one kind of banana is the safest way to farm | They will all turn into apples | The bananas will get too big
test: What would you most likely find if you cut a pear across the middle? | A five-pointed star of seed rooms, like an apple | A stone like a peach | Nothing, pears are solid | Segments like an orange
test: «a strawberry's a berry obviously, it's in the name» What do botanists say? | The red part is the swollen base of the flower, and each outside seed is a tiny fruit | Yes, a strawberry is a true berry like a grape | It is a kind of nut | It is a root
test: «oi, the dog's eating the raisins off the floor!» What should you do? | Tell a grown-up straight away so they can phone the vet | Nothing, because raisins are a healthy treat for dogs | Give the dog more so it is not jealous of you | Give the dog chocolate instead
test: Why does one rotten apple spoil the rest of the bowl? | It gives off lots of ethylene gas that makes the others ripen and rot | Rot jumps from apple to apple like a flea | The rotten apple sucks all the juice out of the other apples through their skins | It does not, that is only a saying
test: If you plant a pineapple top today, about when could you pick your own pineapple? | In about a year and a half or more | Next week | In about a month | In fifty years
test: Your grandparents were born in 1960. How old was the Great Vine then? | 192 years old | 92 years old | 292 years old | 19 years old
test: A grower in Scotland wants new strawberry plants for next spring fast. Which way works quicker, seeds or runners? | Runners, because they already make little plants that root | Seeds, because each strawberry has 200 | Neither, strawberries never make new plants | Planting a whole strawberry
test: What colour would an orange most likely stay in a place with no cool nights? | Green | Purple | Bright red | White
exam: Identify the method by which orchards propagate numerous trees of an identical apple variety. | Grafting, whereby a twig of a desirable tree is joined to young roots | The sowing of pips taken from the desired variety | The burial of halved apples in the soil | The irrigation of saplings with apple juice
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the banana plant? | It is a giant herb whose apparent trunk consists of rolled leaf stalks | It is a woody tree of the palm family | It is a climbing vine supported by tendrils | It is a shrub that bears its fruit beneath the soil
exam: Ascertain the reason for which an unripe banana enclosed in a bag with an apple ripens rapidly. | The apple emits ethylene gas, which accumulates and accelerates ripening | The apple transfers sugar to the banana by contact | Darkness alone causes immediate ripening | The bag raises the temperature to that of boiling
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the consumption of grapes by dogs. | Grapes and raisins may cause serious damage to the kidneys of a dog even in small quantities | Grapes are beneficial to the digestion of dogs | Only green grapes are harmful to dogs | Grapes are harmless to dogs once dried as raisins
